       <div class="col-lg-8">
             <img class="blog-hero-image" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Central Teacher Eligibility Test result 2026">
             <div class="blog-content">
               <div class="blog-body">
                 <h1 class="blog-title text-center mb-4">CTET February 2026 Results OUT: Your Path to a Brighter Teaching Future!</h1>
                 <p>The wait is finally over for lakhs of aspiring teachers! The Central Board of Secondary Education (CBSE) officially declared the results for the Central Teacher Eligibility Test (CTET) February 2026 session on March 30, 2026. This highly anticipated announcement marks a crucial milestone for candidates aiming to secure teaching positions in central government schools across the nation.</p>
                 <p>Conducted on February 7 and 8, 2026, (with a re-exam on March 1 for Paper 2 in specific Bihar centers), the 21st edition of the CTET examination saw a massive participation, underscoring its importance as a mandatory eligibility criterion for teaching Classes 1 to 8.</p>

                 <h2 class="section-title">Key Highlights of CTET February 2026 Results</h2>
                 <div class="subsection-paragraph benefit-card">
                   <p>✅ **Result Declared:** March 30, 2026</p>
                   <p>✅ **Official Website:** ctet.nic.in</p>
                   <p>✅ **Total Registrations:** 26,49,129 candidates</p>
                   <p>✅ **Candidates Appeared:** 23,24,625 candidates</p>
                   <p>✅ **Total Qualified:** 5,97,061 candidates</p>
                   <p>✅ **Overall Pass Percentage:** 25.68%</p>
                   <p>✅ **Certificate Validity:** Lifetime</p>
                 </div>

                 <p class="highlight-text">The CTET is a national-level examination conducted twice a year by CBSE to ascertain the eligibility of candidates for teaching posts. Qualifying this exam opens doors to opportunities in prominent central government schools like KVS and NVS.</p>

                 <h2 class="section-title">How to Check Your CTET February 2026 Result</h2>
                 <p>Checking your CTET result is a straightforward process. Follow these simple steps to access your scorecard:</p>
                 <div class="benefit-item">
                   <div class="benefit-title">1. Visit the Official Website</div>
                   <div class="benefit-content">Navigate to the official CTET website: <a href="https://ctet.nic.in" target="_blank">ctet.nic.in</a>.</div>
                 </div>
                 <div class="benefit-item">
                   <div class="benefit-title">2. Locate the Result Link</div>
                   <div class="benefit-content">On the homepage, look for the 'Candidate Activity' section and click on the "CTET Feb 2026 Result" link.</div>
                 </div>
                 <div class="benefit-item">
                   <div class="benefit-title">3. Enter Login Credentials</div>
                   <div class="benefit-content">A login window will appear. Enter your Roll Number and Date of Birth. Ensure these details match your application form.</div>
                 </div>
                 <div class="benefit-item">
                   <div class="benefit-title">4. View and Download Scorecard</div>
                   <div class="benefit-content">After submitting the details, your CTET February 2026 scorecard will be displayed on the screen. Verify all the information, including your name, category, and marks obtained in both Paper 1 and Paper 2. Download the PDF and take a printout for your records.</div>
                 </div>

                 <h2 class="section-title">CTET February 2026 Examination Statistics</h2>
                 <p>The February 2026 session witnessed significant participation, and the official statistics provide valuable insights into the performance across both papers:</p>
                 <table>
                   <thead>
                     <tr>
                       <th>Paper</th>
                       <th>Total Registered</th>
                       <th>Appeared</th>
                       <th>Qualified</th>
                       <th>Pass Percentage</th>
                     </tr>
                   </thead>
                   <tbody>
                     <tr>
                       <td>Paper 1 (Classes 1-5)</td>
                       <td>12,11,611</td>
                       <td>10,65,410</td>
                       <td>3,58,937</td>
                       <td>33.69%</td>
                     </tr>
                     <tr>
                       <td>Paper 2 (Classes 6-8)</td>
                       <td>21,56,459</td>
                       <td>18,67,428</td>
                       <td>3,46,738</td>
                       <td>18.56%</td>
                     </tr>
                     <tr>
                       <td>**Overall**</td>
                       <td>**26,49,129**</td>
                       <td>**23,24,625**</td>
                       <td>**5,97,061**</td>
                       <td>**25.68%**</td>
                     </tr>
                   </tbody>
                 </table>
                 <p>It's important to note that a candidate could register for both Paper 1 and Paper 2, leading to the total registered candidates being less than the sum of individual paper registrations.</p>

                 <h2 class="section-title">Understanding Qualifying Marks and Certificate</h2>
                 <p>To qualify for the CTET, candidates from the general category must secure a minimum of 60% (90 marks out of 150). For reserved categories (SC, ST, OBC, PwD), the minimum qualifying mark is 55% (82 marks out of 150).</p>
                 <p>Upon qualification, candidates are issued an eligibility certificate. This certificate, along with the marksheet, will be made available digitally on the DigiLocker platform shortly after the result declaration. You can access these documents using your registered mobile number or Aadhaar credentials.</p>
                 <p class="highlight-text">A significant update is that the CTET eligibility certificate is now valid for a lifetime, a change that greatly benefits aspiring teachers by removing the previous re-validation requirements.</p>

                 <h2 class="section-title">What's Next After Qualifying CTET?</h2>
                 <p>Congratulations to all the successful candidates! Clearing CTET is a significant achievement and a gateway to numerous opportunities. Here's what you can expect next:</p>
                 <div class="benefit-item">
                   <div class="benefit-title">1. Apply for Teaching Positions</div>
                   <div class="benefit-content">Qualified candidates are now eligible to apply for teaching vacancies in various central government schools, state government schools, and private institutions for Classes 1 to 8.</div>
                 </div>
                 <div class="benefit-item">
                   <div class="benefit-title">2. Download Digital Certificate from DigiLocker</div>
                   <div class="benefit-content">Your CTET marksheet and eligibility certificate will be uploaded to DigiLocker. Ensure you download and save these digital documents as they are crucial for future recruitment processes.</div>
                 </div>
                 <div class="benefit-item">
                   <div class="benefit-title">3. Stay Updated on Recruitment News</div>
                   <div class="benefit-content">Keep an eye on official notifications from schools and recruiting bodies (like KVS, NVS, Army Public Schools, etc.) for upcoming teaching recruitment drives. Your CTET qualification makes you eligible for these.</div>
                 </div>

                 <p>The declaration of CTET February 2026 results opens new avenues for countless individuals passionate about teaching. We wish all qualified candidates the very best in their journey to build successful and impactful careers in education!</p>
               </div>
             </div>
       </div>

            
            
